Yep. LAWFUL rogue. There's a story behind that.

Notable firsts:
1) First in-game conversion.
2) First use of ring of poly control for protection against poly traps.

3) First use of blessed touchstone to identify gems.
4) First ascent using some other armor besides gray or silver dragon
scale mail.
5) First time enter Gehennom with liches and mind flayers still active.

6) First 3.4.3 ascension (previous ascents were 3.3.0).
7) First ascension using graphics NethackW (previous ascension were
done in text game -- never going back!)
8) First ascension with armor class >-30.

I've been wanting to ascend a rogue since I got my ranger up.  Having
seen the utility of daggers, and remembering that Rogues were issued a
bandolier of the things as well as the ability to get to "expert" in
them, I figured this would be an easy ascension using the Ranger
tactics.

Oh, how we fool ourselves.

I left about a dozen orcish rogue corpses scattered around the dungeon.
 I used orcs for their infravision and their poison resistance. But
even so they seemed to really stink when it came to hit points.
I took a break and tried a human tourist, she did exceptionally well
before being butchered by a grey-elf in the mines (I forgot to watch
the status bar in a fight). I was particularly impressed by the fact
that she tended to have 80 hit points on a level when my orcs still
have 30-35.

I wondered if humans had some sort of advantage re: hit points.  So I
started a human rogue, a person who would be blind in the dark and who
would be vulnerable to poison but would (I hoped) have more hit points.


So I started this one. And as you can  see, went all the way with him.
 He DID seem to have more hit points than my orcs did, although whether
that was coincidence or part of the game, I'm still not sure.

In memory of a certain other burglar, I named him Baggins.
Starting stats:
ST: 10 DX: 18 CO: 17 IN: 9 Wis: 9 Cha: 12

So he's not only weak, he's also stupid. I can see we aren't going to
be reading many books anytime soon.

As an aside, the daggers once again proved their worth, as late into
the game I used them as missile weapons against nymphs and mind flayers
and such that I didn't dare to encounter hand-to-hand.  One good thing
about starting as human was that I started with daggers instead of
orcish daggers, which aren't nearly as effective.

I think what broke this game open was early poison resistance from a
shrieker and a wand of digging, which would make a fairly permanent "E"
word. It saved my life around turn 3600 when I was jumped by two giant
ants and a pony, which would have killed me otherwise.

Found a plate mail and a +3 iron helm, and that solved my AC problems
for the moment.

Turn 8573 -- We've had the discussion before about testing potions by
whapping creatures with them.  There was some concern that I would
whack a critter with a ! of polymorph and deal with something nasty.
It finally happened this game.   I whacked a wolf with a potion of
polymorph. It shuddered and turned into ...

... a piranha.

A piranha on land aint' much. I stood back and let him flop around
awhile before putting him out of his misery with my daggers, which I
was starting to collect.

Found an altar on level 11.  A sac-party gave me Stormbringer, which
proved to be a great asset in the battles ahead.

I started to have problems with rust monsters.   I got tired of the
things, so whenever I met them I would carefully take off my exposed
metal, lay aside Stormbringer, and kill it with my bare hands. I did
this about 5 times.  Turns out rust monsters aren't difficult to kill
bare-handed, and when you don't have any metal for them to touch
there's little they can do.

turn 16174.  sokoban cleared.  bag of holding acquired.

disappointing. I REALLY needed an amulet of reflection.

turn 20864 - then came the accident that changed the course of the
game.
I tried to be very careful in minetown with Stormbringer, not wielding
it unless I absolutely had to.  But I wasn't careful enough --
Itzhak gets angry!

Nooo!!  I wound up killing Itzhak!   Blasted piece of junk!

I almost quit the game right there.  Killing Itzhak is just about
unforgivable in Nethack.  But I decided that I'd come too far to throw
away the character now. I hit on a ... solution ... in which the
character might atone for itself in some limited way ...

But for the present,I had an angry watch on my tail. I had no choice;
I killed them all.  I continued down into the mines and acquired the
luckstone.

turn 23544 -lose +3 helmet to destroy armor.

I also, at some point, acquire a touchstone and bless it, which allows
me to identify gems, luckstones, etc.

turn 29635- teleportitis acquired from Tengu.
Blast it, I wanted teleport control but now I had teleportitis and no
way to control it. Again I was tempted to quit, but decided to play it
out anyway.   I pondered how I could get teleport control and
remembered that the Master Key of Thievery conferred it ...

By this time I was level 14.  I went down towards the quest to see if I
could recover the Key.

And lost my nerve after three levels. reason: I still had no capability
for reflection. I'd encountered wands of death on the quest levels
before and wanted no part of that now.   I figured I would have a
better chance of finding one in the main dungeon, either by silver
dragon death-dropping scales or by shield or by amulet.

turn 39769 - find amulet vs. poison. Try to polymorph it. Get amulet of

change which makes me into a female.

Of course, Baggins caught (or should have) a LOT of heck for this when
(s)he got back to the Thieves guild, but Stormbringer on her belt seems
to have dissuaded the thugs from TOO many comments.

Pushed down through the main dungeon, getting zapped all over the place
by teleportitis, until

turn 41289 - finally find shield of reflection. also boots of
levitation
in pack.

An Aleax courteously donated a shield of reflection.   This was useful,
as it was the ONLY reflection-conferring device I encountered before
the astral plane.

So now I had reflection, but I was still concerned, as I could no
longer two-weapon, after gaining at least skilled level ability in it!
Still, I now had nothing more to fear from the more nasty wands, so
resumed my penetration of the Quest level, pursuing the Master of
Assassins under the town...

turn 42289 - Discover a ring of polymorph control.  I slip it on, as my
greatest fear are poly traps which will ruin the dwarvish mithril I am
now wearing.  This proves to be a good decision, as despite careful
seraching I still stumble into two of them.  The ring of polymorph
control allows me to change into humanoid monsters (Vampire Lord and
"new man", respectively) , thus preserving my armor.

Re-reading, I guess this is where I changed back to male, and why the
second amulet of change made me female again.

turn 43616 - encounter first arch-lich, which turns out to be a
chameleon. Nonetheless, he scared me to death when he warped in next to
me, and me with no magic resistance.

engrave "E" to prevent him from attacking me.This works.  I hit him
with
daggers to bring him down.

Arrive on the lowest level of the quest ... and find no doors over to
the Assassin's area.  I pickaxe the entire wall and find no openings.
Puzzled, I hit the spoilers and discover there are no doors .. "Rogues
are expected to be resourceful".  They offered a few suggestions.

Wearing a ring of polymorph control,  I jumped on a poly trap and chose
to become a Xorn (after taking off my armor, of course).  I then phased
through the wall and used a ring of conflict to get the monsters
fighting each other instead of me.

Turn 46366 -  Face the master of Assassins.  I start with a volley of
+2 daggers.   He jumps out.   I go to pick up my daggers. When he jumps
back in, he hits HARD and knocks my Xorn form down by quite a few
points.   I slide on a ring of free action and break a ! o paralysis
over his head.   He stiffens up.  His eyes grow wide with terror as I
show him the black, rune-covered blade that was his doom and slid it
into his chest.

Turn 47928 - While clearing Fort Ludios, I get stressed while carrying
a chest and inadvertently step into the moat.  Not only do I get a lot
of stuff rusty but I have to leave stuff in the water in order to
struggle out.  I lose my touchstone and the chest.   I consider
salvaging it with my ring of poly control but decide against it. It
stays in the water until game end.

turn 50373 - A red dragon drops the first dragon scales I see.   I use
scroll of enchant armor to make red dragon scale mail, because it's far
less heavy than the mithril I am wearing.  I figure I'll swap it for
gray or silver dragon scale mail taken after killing one of said
dragons -- only it doesn't happen because I only encounter one or two
of those dragons, and they do not leave scales. I wind up wearing this
red dragon armor for the rest of the game.

50742 - master mindflayer destroyed with daggers.
50825 - Schrodinger's cat is dead in level 25 treasure zoo. Har har.

51980 - kill Medusa by letting her see her reflection. Leave Perseus
statue intact in hope of finding stone-to-flesh.  Since there is a
chance of restoring him to life, I will not destroy his statue.

53390 - crack passtune of castle using bugle. There are 3 liches and
demiliches inside.  One is killed by the drawbridge (along with many
other creatures). As for the rest ... I slide on a ring of conflict and
watch the soldiers fight among themselves. It turns out that even an
all-powerful lich isn't a match for 8 soldiers all whacking him at the
same time.

53754 - 3 wishes in wand of wishing. Wish for 1) cloak of magic
resistance 2) blessed +2 magic bane. 3) scrolls of genocide. Re-charge
and wish for blessed book of identify, one other thing which I forget.
I'm left with one charge left.

54000 -- it as at this point that my character attempts to atone for
his murder of Itzhak.  He is not happy with Stormbringer -- the vile,
bloodthirsty blade that steals the souls of it's targets. That killed
the peaceful Itzhak, though he meant no harm.   Baggins is not happy
either with the blade or with the deity who approves of it -- his
greatest and best gift.

So we head up for a lawful altar on level 24 with a lawful priest. We
had already bought some protection from him, and I want him to see
this. Besides, this sort of thing SHOULD be done with  a priest
present, don't you think?

I read a cursed scroll of genocide and reverse-genocide black unicorns.
 I kill one with Stormbringer and lay it on the altar.
<Chaotic> gets angry!  <Lawful> accepts your allegiance.

Of course,  I've lost some luck and my alignment is bad.  No problem! I
know where I can find a whole bunch of black unicorns to put on the
altar.  This ensures I feel "appropriately lawful" and perhaps
contributes to final reconciliation with the new deity -- that and a
few wands of create monster.  I also hunt down white unicorns and toss
some of my large stash of gems at my new allies, returning my luck to
it's normal level.

As a token of her new allegiance, Baggins puts stormbringer away, never
to use it again. Although she does lapse a few times, after a few tries
Stormbringer goes into the pack and stays there for the duration of the
game. As an act of faith, for much of the rest of the game Baggins uses
an ordinary silver saber acquired from a captain.

Baggins considers throwing Stormbringer into the water on the Medusa
level, but then realizes the evil blade will only find it's way back to
the world of men and claim more victims. Accordingly , he resolves to
carry it with him to the end game and when he ascends to hand it over.
There, in the Heavens, perhaps they can find a way to destroy it If
not, there it will be kept safe, unable to harm men. Perhaps they can
even find a way to unmake it, freeing Itzhak and countless others from
the blade.

All creatures generated up to this point retain their original feelings
toward me, but all subsequently generated chaotic creatures (such as
elves) are hostile and lawful creatures (such as dwarves, though there
are exceptions) are peaceful.

As an aside, the creation of hostile elves allows me to finally gain
sleep resistance.  Not easy to do when their friendly.   Baggins didn't
make much of a chaotic rogue, anyway .. He actually BOUGHT stuff in
shops despite the fact that no self-respecting rogue would ever buy
what he could steal!   At the time, he rationalized that he had enough
problems stealing the Amulet (the heist of a lifetime) without dealing
with shopkeepers or kops.  But that was an excuse -- the real reason is
that he just didn't like stealing from innocents.

56419 -- while clearing the Valley of the Dead,  get a "you violate the
sanctity of this grave!" message -- that's right, the character's
lawful now, and that means he suffers penalties for grave digging.
Since I fear the penalties, I use an "object detect" effect to see if I
can find anything under the tombs and only dig those up.

9388 - Asmodeus bought off.
60719 - Announce presence on Juiblex level with bugle. Engrave E on
stair
first. He warps in and I kill him with +2 silver saber.

62829 -  at this point, having read the message about "more stuff on
Orcus level if m is genocided",  I burn my last wishes, exhausting the
wand, for two magic markers.  It's just as well, as these are the only
magic markers I see in the entire game, in any branch, on any level.

After improving my armor, I go on a genociding spree,  wiping out L,
mind flayer, master mind flayer, all eels, and all mimics.

It turns out that the rumor doesn't appear to be true ... there were
very few bits of equipment in Orcus Town.  It's just that instead of
having shops filled with mimics, there were empty shops with a  very
few things lying around. Still, I got a gauntlets of Power out of the
deal, so it could have been worse.

64284 - kill baalzebub with potion of paralysis and silver saber.

70305 - kill orcus.It's a goat rope of a battle.I open it with a wand
of digging,
and he counters with a wand of death which reflects from my shield. I
fire daggers.
I try to smack him with a potion of paralyis but he has already ported
away, and I nail a
stalker instead. I take out my next two potions, but they get dropped
down
the stairs and crash.  I levelport, and succeed on my second try. I
teleport to the level above,
then come down and meet Orcus on the stairs. Smack him to bits with a
silver saber.  Recover his wand of death with 5 charges in it.

78360 - locate vibrating square on level 50 and mark with tinning kit.

turn 82443 - clear vlad's tower.
At this point I am perturbed because my shield of reflection prevents
two-weaponing, and I really need to do that.  I collect several amulets
through the course of the game and begin polypiling.  This and previous
polypiling gives me 4 amulets of life saving.  Nonetheless, I need
reflection more than life saving so I polypile two the life savers and
finally get an amulet of reflection, allowing me to stow my shield in
the bag of holding.  I now imitate Drizzt Dourden, two-weaponing a pair
of silver sabers (one +3) .

I also acquire an amulet of change. Not understanding the import of the
"You feel like a new man" message,  I put it on expecting to change
from FEMALE to MALE, and instead wind up changing from MALE to FEMALE.
Ah well... I kept this form for the duration of the game.

I carry a halberd which I use to dispatch disenchanters.  Being
unenchanted, it takes no hurt from their attacks while allowing me to
keep them away from my armor. After I acquire a couple wands of
cancellation,  I stow the halberd and use the wand of cancellation to
defeat the disenchanter's attacks. I then wade in with melee weapons
and take it apart.

turn 85072 - Realizing that my silver saber would not work well against
the Angels and priests in the end game, I decide I need more punch. But
sac parties haven't brought anything ... can you get gifts if you
convert?  Wasn't sure.   And I might easily get a piece of garbage.
Then I remembered... I was LAWFUL!  That means I am eligible for a
moderately good artifact which, although not great, is still better
than nothing. I recover a long sword from a Nazgul I had killed and dip
it in a fountain on Juibilex' level.

"A hand reaches up to bless the sword..."

Yup.  I am now two-weaponing the +0 blessed Rustproof Excalibur along
with my +3 silver saber.  It later gets bumped to +3 by scavenged
scrolls of enchant weapon, but this weapon combination remains my main
tools for the rest of the game.


The game gave me NO magic markers and NO dragon scale armor, but it
gave me SEVEN wands of polymorph. Taking the hint,  I began polypiling
massively in the hopes of getting a magic marker and dragon scale armor
and did not. I did, however, get a Hawaiian shirt out of the deal.   I
wonder if magic markers cannot be polypiled?  Or was I just unlucky?

In any case, I gathered up as much money as I could and bought as much
protection as I reasonably could (10 points).  I dried up every single
fountain in the dungeon, attracting three water demons, but got no
wishes.  Didn't find a single magic lamp.  At last, I burned out my
last magic marker on scrolls of gold detection and enchant armor,
bringing my AC to -27.  I found this disturbingly high, but remembering
that some have done it with -21 I decided to give it a try.

I also go nurse dancing, but I have some very close calls as
chickatrices et al walk up to me while I'm naked.  What's worse is that
I almost lash out by reflex, forgetting that I'm barehanded without
gloves ... but I catch myself in time. After that, I decide that
nurse-dancing is simply too dangerous, and after my HP is up to 345 I
desist and head on.

turn 85688 - inherent teleport control acquired from enemy Tengu.
This tengu was generated on the castle level while I was still chaotic.
 Not really necessary, since the Key conferred it, but it was still
nice to have it inherently.

Incidentally, the Key blasted me every time I picked it up and every
time I used it, for 20 HP a shot.  I learned to be careful of it,
although it still whispered rumors as before.

I portaged up a whole bunch of potions, eventually making some 65
potions of holy water.  I figured that would be enough ...

that is, until a mountain centaur came along and tried to zap me with a
wand of striking.  It missed me, but it did blast 10 potions I had
dropped, and suddenly I was down to 55 potions.  Still enough, I
thought.  I carried 20 of them with me into Gehennom (knowing how much
it hurt lawful characters) but I figured Magic bane would also help
with that. I left most everything else in the Castle on level 29.

turn 90149 - Stupidly walk into the  moat in front of the wizard's
tower.  No permanent damage, except it rusted my pickaxe and diluted my
14 potions of full healing.

The next turn I almost killed myself by trying to eat an umber hulk
AFTER I was satiated from eating a dragon.  When it gave me the "stop
eating" prompt I was VERY careful to make sure I wanted to press "y"
and watched VERY closely to make sure that was what I pressed ...
*phew*. After this I tried to get a grip on myself.

90311 - Find an ice box containing a cockatrice corpse.   I equip it
and go to visit Rodney.   He curses me once, but I bean him with the
